userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using Chart.js.

CONCEPT: Amplitude modulation encodes a signal by changing a carrier wave's height, while frequency modulation encodes the same signal by changing the carrier wave's spacing.
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): AM changes carrier amplitude while keeping frequency nearly constant; FM changes carrier frequency while keeping amplitude nearly constant.
DEMO STEPS: 4 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "Amplitude modulation encodes a signal by changing a carrier wave's height, while frequency modulation encodes the same signal by changing the carrier wave's spacing.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "shared horizontal time direction across all three stacked traces",
    "top black low-frequency information waveform with two broad crests and one trough",
    "right-side label 'Signal' aligned with the top waveform",
    "middle red high-frequency AM carrier trace",
    "AM trace with uniform peak spacing across time",
    "AM trace with varying amplitude envelope that follows the black signal shape",
    "right-side label 'AM' aligned with the middle waveform",
    "bottom blue FM carrier trace",
    "FM trace with constant peak-to-trough height across time",
    "FM trace with compressed cycle spacing under signal crests and stretched cycle spacing under the signal trough",
    "right-side label 'FM' aligned with the bottom waveform",
    "vertical time marker crossing all three traces",
    "time-marker readout showing signal height, AM envelope height, and FM cycle spacing",
    "active-carrier highlight applied to either the AM or FM trace",
    "extracted black signal reconstruction displayed above the active carrier during decoding"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "timeScan",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Scan time",
      "range": [
        0,
        1,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 0.25,
      "teaches": "At any instant, the same signal value appears as AM envelope height but as FM cycle spacing, so the message is stored in different wave properties"
    },
    {
      "id": "decodingMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Decode carrier",
      "options": [
        "AM",
        "FM"
      ],
      "default": "AM",
      "teaches": "Recovering the message from AM means reading amplitude changes, while recovering it from FM means reading frequency changes"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Compare the three traces",
      "narration": "The black curve is the slow signal to be transmitted. The red AM and blue FM traces are faster carrier waves that encode that same signal in different ways.",
      "state": {
        "timeScan": 0.25,
        "decodingMode": "AM"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Scan an AM crest",
      "narration": "Move the time marker under a high part of the signal. The AM wave has a large envelope height there, while its peak spacing stays uniform.",
      "state": {
        "timeScan": 0.18,
        "decodingMode": "AM"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Scan the signal trough",
      "narration": "At the low part of the signal, the AM envelope shrinks. In contrast, the FM wave keeps the same height but its cycles spread farther apart.",
      "state": {
        "timeScan": 0.5,
        "decodingMode": "FM"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Decode the same message two ways",
      "narration": "Switch the decoding mode: AM reconstructs the signal from envelope height, while FM reconstructs it from changing cycle spacing. Both carriers can recover the same black signal curve.",
      "state": {
        "timeScan": 0.75,
        "decodingMode": "FM"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "AM changes carrier amplitude while keeping frequency nearly constant; FM changes carrier frequency while keeping amplitude nearly constant.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a clean white background with three horizontally aligned waveform bands stacked vertically and no visible axes or grid. The top signal should be a smooth black sinusoidal-like curve with two broad crests and one trough. The middle AM trace should be red, high frequency, and evenly spaced, with its upper and lower envelope swelling beneath the signal crests and narrowing beneath the signal trough. The bottom FM trace should be blue with constant vertical height; its cycles should be tightly packed beneath signal crests and widely spaced beneath the trough. Place the labels 'Signal', 'AM', and 'FM' at the far right of their respective traces. The slider should draw a vertical marker through all three traces and show concise numeric or qualitative readouts such as high/medium/low signal height, AM envelope height, and FM spacing. The decoding toggle should visually emphasize the selected carrier and draw an extracted black signal curve above or near it to show what property is being read."
}

